---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import State from '../components/State.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import FundingChart from '../components/FundingChart.astro';
import { site } from '../config';
---
<Base
  title="What the public record shows about donor influence and access at Harvard"
  description="An independent, public-source review of donor influence, introduced funding, post-conviction campus access and campus safety at Harvard University. Every claim is cited and graded."
  eyebrow="Independent public-source review"
  lede="This site collects what official reports, court filings and established reporting document about one donor’s access to Harvard after a criminal conviction, the money that followed his introductions, and how the University responded. It is written for prospective students, students, alumni and researchers who want the record without the noise."
  reviewed="2026-11-20"
  toc={false}
  article={false}
>
  <ul class="figures full" aria-label="Headline figures from Harvard’s own review">
    <li><span class="num">$9.1M</span><span class="lbl">Epstein gifts to Harvard, 1998 to 2008<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></span></li>
    <li><span class="num">$0</span><span class="lbl">Gifts Harvard accepted from him after the 2008 conviction<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></span></li>
    <li><span class="num">40+</span><span class="lbl">Visits to one research program’s offices, 2010 to 2018<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></span></li>
    <li><span class="num">$9.5M</span><span class="lbl">Given by donors he introduced, 2010 to 2015<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></span></li>
  </ul>

  <p class="sans small"><a href="/start/">Start here: reading paths for prospective students, students, alumni and researchers</a> · <a href="/briefing/">The record on one page</a></p>

  <h2 id="scope">Scope</h2>
  <p>
    Jeffrey Epstein was convicted in Florida in 2008. Harvard stopped taking his money that year. Its own 2020 review
    found that he nonetheless kept physical access to a research program he had funded, visited it dozens of times, and
    introduced other donors whose gifts reached the same faculty.<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /> A large Department of
    Justice document release in early 2026 added detail and prompted Harvard to reopen and widen its review.<Cite id="hm-2026-probe" />
  </p>
  <p>
    The pages below set out that record section by section. They cover what happened at Harvard, how it compares with
    the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, and, separately, the University’s public record on sexual misconduct and campus safety.
  </p>

  <h2 id="money">The documented money, on one scale</h2>
  <p>
    Harvard stopped taking Epstein’s money in 2008. What continued came through other donors he had
    introduced. MIT, by contrast, accepted gifts directly after the conviction. Caveats are on the
    <a href="/donor-pathways/">donor pathways</a> page.
  </p>
  <FundingChart />

  <h2 id="how-to-read">How to read this site</h2>
  <p>Three rules apply on every page.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Every factual claim has a numbered source.</strong> Sources carry a reliability grade from A1 (primary official record) to B2 (thin or unverified). The <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> explains the grades.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Three states are kept apart.</strong> A plan is not an act, and an act is not a result.
      <span style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.4rem 1.2rem;margin-top:.5rem"><State s="intent" /><State s="activity" /><State s="verified" /></span>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Nothing goes beyond the public record.</strong> Where the record is silent, the page says so. Being named here does not imply wrongdoing unless a cited record says so.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="findings">Key findings</h2>
  <ul class="cards full">
    <li><a class="card" href="/program-for-evolutionary-dynamics/"><h3>Program for Evolutionary Dynamics</h3><p>A $6.5 million founding gift, a key card and an office, more than 40 visits after the conviction, and a program that Harvard closed in 2021.</p><span class="more">Timeline and sources</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/harvard-medical-school/"><h3>Harvard Medical School, Church and Thakuria</h3><p>Introduced-donor gifts, enrolment in a genome study, a 2013 dispute over a tissue sample, and a 2014 invoice whose larger proposals were never carried out.</p><span class="more">Confirmed activity and unexecuted proposals</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/donor-pathways/"><h3>Donor influence and introduced funding</h3><p>How $9.5 million reached two faculty members from donors Epstein introduced after Harvard had stopped accepting his own gifts.</p><span class="more">The introduced-donor pattern</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/dna-genetics/"><h3>DNA and genetics record</h3><p>One small sequencing job, consumer test kits sent as gifts, a business description later alleged to be false, and a second-hand account of a plan never carried out.</p><span class="more">Claim-by-claim ledger</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/campus-safety/"><h3>Campus safety and sexual misconduct</h3><p>Federal findings, climate surveys, crime statistics and the documented outcomes of faculty cases, with where to find the current numbers.</p><span class="more">Public-record summary</span></a></li>
    <li><a class="card" href="/comparison/"><h3>Cross-institutional comparison</h3><p>Harvard’s program, MIT’s Media Lab and Harvard Medical School side by side on funding, access, DNA activity and institutional response.</p><span class="more">Comparison table</span></a></li>
  </ul>

  <Callout title="What this site does not claim" plain>
    <p>
      The public record does not show a joint research output, a shared genetic database, or any operational
      programme built on the DNA-related material. It shows access, introduced money and stated interest. The pages
      here are written to keep that distinction visible.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="status">Status of this edition</h2>
  <p>
    This is a first edition. Source entries marked “pending check” were compiled from published summaries and are
    being re-read against the original documents. If you find an error, <a href="/about/#corrections">tell us</a>;
    corrections are logged publicly on the <a href="/updates/">update log</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="small muted sans">{site.disclaimerShort}</p>
</Base>
